1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,018
Every time you ask an AI chatbot

2
00:00:03,018 --> 00:00:04,059
a question,

3
00:00:04,059 --> 00:00:07,060
a machine somewhere does the math.

4
00:00:07,310 --> 00:00:10,420
Behind the apps are the AI labs.

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,710
Behind the labs are the cloud companies

6
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:14,660
and the data centers.

7
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:17,091
And behind almost all of them,

8
00:00:17,091 --> 00:00:18,950
there is one supplier.

9
00:00:24,183 --> 00:00:26,637
In October twenty twenty-five,

10
00:00:26,637 --> 00:00:27,603
that supplier,

11
00:00:27,603 --> 00:00:28,322
Nvidia,

12
00:00:28,322 --> 00:00:31,296
became the first company in history to

13
00:00:31,296 --> 00:00:34,275
be worth more than 5 trillion dollars.

14
00:00:34,517 --> 00:00:36,854
In its last full fiscal year,

15
00:00:36,854 --> 00:00:41,559
it sold 215.9 billion dollars worth of

16
00:00:41,559 --> 00:00:42,417
products,

17
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:45,080
about eight times what it sold just

18
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:46,863
three years earlier.

19
00:00:47,117 --> 00:00:48,737
For most of its life,

20
00:00:48,737 --> 00:00:51,482
Nvidia was known for video game graphics

21
00:00:51,482 --> 00:00:52,357
cards.

22
00:00:52,447 --> 00:00:54,297
So how did a gaming company end

23
00:00:54,297 --> 00:00:55,777
up at the center of the AI

24
00:00:55,777 --> 00:00:57,117
economy?

25
00:00:57,297 --> 00:00:58,937
And can it stay there?

26
00:00:59,117 --> 00:01:01,951
This is The Hidden Economics.

27
00:01:07,617 --> 00:01:11,947
Nvidia was founded in 1993 in California

28
00:01:11,947 --> 00:01:14,908
by Jensen Huang and two fellow engineers,

29
00:01:14,908 --> 00:01:17,747
to make chips for computer graphics.

30
00:01:18,117 --> 00:01:20,065
In 1999,

31
00:01:20,065 --> 00:01:21,943
it launched what it calls the first

32
00:01:21,943 --> 00:01:22,389
GPU,

33
00:01:22,389 --> 00:01:24,867
a graphics processing unit.

34
00:01:25,087 --> 00:01:28,395
Its business was drawing video games,

35
00:01:28,395 --> 00:01:29,273
fast.

36
00:01:29,517 --> 00:01:31,927
Here is the key idea.

37
00:01:32,217 --> 00:01:34,040
To draw one frame of a game,

38
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,118
a chip has to color millions of

39
00:01:36,118 --> 00:01:36,787
pixels,

40
00:01:36,787 --> 00:01:38,865
and each pixel can be worked out

41
00:01:38,865 --> 00:01:40,157
on its own.

42
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:42,629
So instead of a few very fast

43
00:01:42,629 --> 00:01:43,307
workers,

44
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:45,643
a graphics chip uses a huge number

45
00:01:45,643 --> 00:01:47,021
of simpler ones,

46
00:01:47,021 --> 00:01:49,256
all working at the same time.

47
00:01:49,517 --> 00:01:51,457
Give both kinds of chip the same

48
00:01:51,457 --> 00:01:52,067
job:

49
00:01:52,067 --> 00:01:54,284
a pile of small tasks that don't

50
00:01:54,284 --> 00:01:56,547
depend on each other.

51
00:01:56,817 --> 00:01:59,717
The CPU's few powerful cores work through

52
00:01:59,717 --> 00:02:01,187
them in rounds.

53
00:02:01,327 --> 00:02:03,223
The GPU gives one to each of

54
00:02:03,223 --> 00:02:04,621
its many cores,

55
00:02:04,621 --> 00:02:07,117
and finishes in a few steps.

56
00:02:07,117 --> 00:02:08,777
But flip the problem.

57
00:02:08,837 --> 00:02:10,546
In a step-by-step job,

58
00:02:10,546 --> 00:02:12,527
where each step needs the result of

59
00:02:12,527 --> 00:02:13,493
the last one,

60
00:02:13,493 --> 00:02:15,787
the CPU's faster cores win.

61
00:02:15,787 --> 00:02:18,135
Real performance always depends on the

62
00:02:18,135 --> 00:02:19,204
workload.

63
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:22,047
And training a neural network turns out

64
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:24,176
to look a lot like graphics:

65
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:27,551
enormous piles of multiplications that can

66
00:02:27,551 --> 00:02:29,190
run side by side.

67
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,356
In 2012,

68
00:02:31,356 --> 00:02:33,294
a network called AlexNet,

69
00:02:33,294 --> 00:02:35,448
trained on two Nvidia gaming cards,

70
00:02:35,448 --> 00:02:38,660
won a famous image recognition contest.

71
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,496
Researchers took notice,

72
00:02:40,496 --> 00:02:42,340
and so did Nvidia.

73
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:44,734
In 2017,

74
00:02:44,734 --> 00:02:47,102
it launched its first chip with Tensor

75
00:02:47,102 --> 00:02:47,866
Cores,

76
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:50,308
built from the ground up for AI.

77
00:02:55,950 --> 00:02:58,030
But the chip was only half the

78
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:59,150
story.

79
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:01,022
Years before AI took off,

80
00:03:01,022 --> 00:03:02,998
in 2006,

81
00:03:02,998 --> 00:03:04,878
Nvidia released CUDA,

82
00:03:04,878 --> 00:03:07,020
software that let programmers use its

83
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:10,000
graphics chips for general computing.

84
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:11,304
On top of it,

85
00:03:11,304 --> 00:03:13,347
Nvidia built libraries for math,

86
00:03:13,347 --> 00:03:15,642
for neural networks and for linking chips

87
00:03:15,642 --> 00:03:16,930
together.

88
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,690
The popular AI frameworks plugged into

89
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:21,000
those libraries.

90
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,640
Developers learned them.

91
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:25,170
And applications were built on top.

92
00:03:25,170 --> 00:03:28,786
Today Nvidia says more than 7.5 million

93
00:03:28,786 --> 00:03:32,552
developers use CUDA and its other tools.

94
00:03:32,817 --> 00:03:35,255
That creates what economists call

95
00:03:35,255 --> 00:03:36,667
switching costs.

96
00:03:36,767 --> 00:03:39,673
A company can move to another chip,

97
00:03:39,673 --> 00:03:41,383
but first it may have to port

98
00:03:41,383 --> 00:03:42,824
and retest its code,

99
00:03:42,824 --> 00:03:44,753
tune its performance again,

100
00:03:44,753 --> 00:03:45,706
change its tools,

101
00:03:45,706 --> 00:03:47,527
and retrain its teams.

102
00:03:47,597 --> 00:03:49,477
None of this is impossible.

103
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:52,067
Other chips run AI every day.

104
00:03:52,157 --> 00:03:55,041
But every step costs time and money,

105
00:03:55,041 --> 00:03:58,551
and that friction is valuable to Nvidia.

106
00:03:58,817 --> 00:04:01,887
It is also a network effect.

107
00:04:01,947 --> 00:04:05,093
More developers write more software for

108
00:04:05,093 --> 00:04:06,637
Nvidia chips.

109
00:04:06,797 --> 00:04:10,057
More software attracts more customers.

110
00:04:10,147 --> 00:04:12,815
More customers bring in more money for

111
00:04:12,815 --> 00:04:14,389
new chips and new tools,

112
00:04:14,389 --> 00:04:17,567
which attract even more developers.

113
00:04:17,567 --> 00:04:20,183
The wheel feeds itself.

114
00:04:25,817 --> 00:04:26,379
Now,

115
00:04:26,379 --> 00:04:27,867
the money.

116
00:04:27,957 --> 00:04:30,434
Nvidia's year ends in late January,

117
00:04:30,434 --> 00:04:33,436
so its fiscal twenty twenty-six is mostly

118
00:04:33,436 --> 00:04:36,037
calendar twenty twenty-five.

119
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:37,431
For a decade,

120
00:04:37,431 --> 00:04:39,567
revenue grew steadily.

121
00:04:39,737 --> 00:04:41,261
Then it exploded:

122
00:04:41,261 --> 00:04:44,506
from 27 billion dollars in fiscal twenty

123
00:04:44,506 --> 00:04:45,465
twenty-three,

124
00:04:45,465 --> 00:04:49,865
to 215.9 billion dollars in fiscal twenty

125
00:04:49,865 --> 00:04:51,203
twenty-six.

126
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:54,210
What changed is who buys.

127
00:04:54,250 --> 00:04:55,855
In fiscal twenty twenty,

128
00:04:55,855 --> 00:04:57,866
gaming was the biggest business,

129
00:04:57,866 --> 00:05:00,101
and data center sales were about a

130
00:05:00,101 --> 00:05:01,920
quarter of the total.

131
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:04,111
By fiscal twenty twenty-six,

132
00:05:04,111 --> 00:05:08,080
data center revenue had reached 193.7

133
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:09,620
billion dollars.

134
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:12,779
That is about 90 percent of everything

135
00:05:12,779 --> 00:05:14,008
Nvidia sold.

136
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:16,200
Those buyers are cloud giants,

137
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,506
AI labs and large companies building what

138
00:05:19,506 --> 00:05:22,400
Nvidia calls AI factories.

139
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,350
And they are concentrated.

140
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:26,305
In fiscal twenty twenty-six,

141
00:05:26,305 --> 00:05:29,205
one direct customer accounted for 22

142
00:05:29,205 --> 00:05:30,940
percent of Nvidia's revenue,

143
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:33,781
and another for 14 percent.

144
00:05:34,017 --> 00:05:36,447
Then look at the margin.

145
00:05:36,767 --> 00:05:38,610
Gross margin is what is left from

146
00:05:38,610 --> 00:05:40,915
each dollar of sales after the cost

147
00:05:40,915 --> 00:05:42,897
of making the product.

148
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:46,330
Nvidia's rose from about 57 percent in

149
00:05:46,330 --> 00:05:49,665
fiscal twenty twenty-three to 75 percent

150
00:05:49,665 --> 00:05:52,287
in fiscal twenty twenty-five.

151
00:05:52,737 --> 00:05:55,720
Last year it slipped to 71 percent,

152
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:59,344
partly because of a 4.5 billion dollar

153
00:05:59,344 --> 00:06:01,317
charge on chips it could no longer

154
00:06:01,317 --> 00:06:02,903
sell to China.

155
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:05,310
Why so profitable?

156
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:08,200
Demand ran far ahead of supply.

157
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:10,670
The product was hard to replace.

158
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:13,327
And customers were not just buying chips,

159
00:06:13,327 --> 00:06:14,903
but whole systems:

160
00:06:14,903 --> 00:06:15,894
processors,

161
00:06:15,894 --> 00:06:17,927
networking and software that work

162
00:06:17,927 --> 00:06:19,290
together.

163
00:06:19,550 --> 00:06:22,526
Networking alone brought in 31 billion

164
00:06:22,526 --> 00:06:24,590
dollars last fiscal year.

165
00:06:24,780 --> 00:06:26,782
And Nvidia keeps reinvesting:

166
00:06:26,782 --> 00:06:28,491
it says it has spent more than

167
00:06:28,491 --> 00:06:31,226
76 billion dollars on research and

168
00:06:31,226 --> 00:06:34,024
development since it was founded.

169
00:06:34,283 --> 00:06:36,273
And it is still growing.

170
00:06:36,413 --> 00:06:38,606
In the quarter that ended in July

171
00:06:38,606 --> 00:06:39,899
twenty twenty-six,

172
00:06:39,899 --> 00:06:43,232
Nvidia brought in 96.2 billion dollars,

173
00:06:43,232 --> 00:06:46,236
up 106 percent from a year earlier,

174
00:06:46,236 --> 00:06:49,701
and it expected about 108 billion dollars

175
00:06:49,701 --> 00:06:51,836
in the quarter after that.

176
00:06:57,483 --> 00:06:59,943
Here is the surprising part.

177
00:07:00,133 --> 00:07:02,091
Nvidia designs its chips,

178
00:07:02,091 --> 00:07:04,393
but it does not make them.

179
00:07:04,573 --> 00:07:07,489
Its engineers in California send the

180
00:07:07,489 --> 00:07:09,582
designs to TSMC in Taiwan,

181
00:07:09,582 --> 00:07:12,833
which prints them onto silicon wafers.

182
00:07:13,293 --> 00:07:15,418
The chips are then joined with stacks

183
00:07:15,418 --> 00:07:18,580
of high bandwidth memory from SK Hynix,

184
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:20,148
Micron and Samsung,

185
00:07:20,148 --> 00:07:23,070
using an advanced packaging technique

186
00:07:23,070 --> 00:07:24,733
called CoWoS.

187
00:07:25,323 --> 00:07:28,206
Contract manufacturers assemble them into

188
00:07:28,206 --> 00:07:28,849
servers,

189
00:07:28,849 --> 00:07:31,067
and the servers ship to data centers

190
00:07:31,067 --> 00:07:32,554
around the world.

191
00:07:32,817 --> 00:07:35,160
A chain like this is only as

192
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:37,517
fast as its slowest link.

193
00:07:37,537 --> 00:07:40,181
Nvidia's own annual report warns that a

194
00:07:40,181 --> 00:07:43,089
shortage of any one component can hold

195
00:07:43,089 --> 00:07:44,747
back its sales.

196
00:07:44,967 --> 00:07:47,944
When advanced packaging or memory runs

197
00:07:47,944 --> 00:07:48,416
short,

198
00:07:48,416 --> 00:07:51,249
everything downstream has to wait.

199
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:54,245
Most of this chain is concentrated in

200
00:07:54,245 --> 00:07:55,046
Asia,

201
00:07:55,046 --> 00:07:57,303
above all in Taiwan.

202
00:07:57,363 --> 00:08:00,143
Nvidia is trying to spread it out.

203
00:08:00,503 --> 00:08:02,978
In October twenty twenty-five,

204
00:08:02,978 --> 00:08:05,478
it showed the first Blackwell wafer made

205
00:08:05,478 --> 00:08:07,453
in the United States,

206
00:08:07,453 --> 00:08:10,553
at TSMC's factory in Arizona.

207
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:13,581
But reports say those wafers still travel

208
00:08:13,581 --> 00:08:16,654
back to Taiwan to be packaged.

209
00:08:22,317 --> 00:08:24,867
So who could challenge Nvidia?

210
00:08:24,977 --> 00:08:27,598
Its annual report names them:

211
00:08:27,598 --> 00:08:29,278
chipmakers like AMD,

212
00:08:29,278 --> 00:08:30,959
Intel and Huawei,

213
00:08:30,959 --> 00:08:33,522
and also some of its own biggest

214
00:08:33,522 --> 00:08:34,977
customers.

215
00:08:35,147 --> 00:08:35,964
Google,

216
00:08:35,964 --> 00:08:39,344
Amazon and Microsoft all design AI chips

217
00:08:39,344 --> 00:08:40,790
of their own.

218
00:08:41,050 --> 00:08:43,651
AMD sells data center GPUs too,

219
00:08:43,651 --> 00:08:46,543
with its own open software platform,

220
00:08:46,543 --> 00:08:47,740
called ROCm.

221
00:08:47,740 --> 00:08:50,503
In calendar twenty twenty-five,

222
00:08:50,503 --> 00:08:53,545
AMD's whole data center business,

223
00:08:53,545 --> 00:08:56,307
including its server processors,

224
00:08:56,307 --> 00:09:00,340
brought in 16.6 billion dollars.

225
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:03,481
Nvidia's data center revenue in its latest

226
00:09:03,481 --> 00:09:07,978
fiscal year was 193.7 billion dollars.

227
00:09:08,217 --> 00:09:10,877
Then there are custom chips.

228
00:09:10,977 --> 00:09:12,626
Google builds its own TPUs,

229
00:09:12,626 --> 00:09:15,377
now in their seventh-generation.

230
00:09:15,497 --> 00:09:17,209
Amazon builds Trainium,

231
00:09:17,209 --> 00:09:19,442
and its Project Rainier cluster runs

232
00:09:19,442 --> 00:09:22,027
nearly half a million of them.

233
00:09:22,257 --> 00:09:24,578
In late twenty twenty-five,

234
00:09:24,578 --> 00:09:27,223
the AI lab Anthropic said it plans

235
00:09:27,223 --> 00:09:29,868
to use up to one million Google

236
00:09:29,868 --> 00:09:30,426
TPUs,

237
00:09:30,426 --> 00:09:33,365
while still using Amazon's chips and

238
00:09:33,365 --> 00:09:34,846
Nvidia's GPUs.

239
00:09:35,083 --> 00:09:37,493
Why build your own chip?

240
00:09:37,493 --> 00:09:38,883
Economics.

241
00:09:38,903 --> 00:09:41,301
A general-purpose GPU can run almost any

242
00:09:41,301 --> 00:09:42,021
AI workload,

243
00:09:42,021 --> 00:09:43,663
has the widest software support,

244
00:09:43,663 --> 00:09:45,813
and can be rented from many clouds.

245
00:09:45,813 --> 00:09:50,233
A custom accelerator is designed around

246
00:09:50,233 --> 00:09:53,043
one company's own workloads.

247
00:09:53,173 --> 00:09:55,391
At enormous scale it can be cheaper

248
00:09:55,391 --> 00:09:55,976
to run,

249
00:09:55,976 --> 00:09:58,396
but it is mostly available inside that

250
00:09:58,396 --> 00:09:59,385
company's cloud,

251
00:09:59,385 --> 00:10:01,401
and software has to be adapted to

252
00:10:01,401 --> 00:10:02,203
it.

253
00:10:02,573 --> 00:10:05,323
Different jobs favor different chips.

254
00:10:05,453 --> 00:10:07,575
There is no single winner.

255
00:10:13,217 --> 00:10:16,152
The whole boom rests on one thing:

256
00:10:16,152 --> 00:10:18,347
customers spending.

257
00:10:18,537 --> 00:10:20,863
Trackers estimate that Amazon,

258
00:10:20,863 --> 00:10:21,780
Microsoft,

259
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:24,365
Alphabet and Meta plan to spend roughly

260
00:10:24,365 --> 00:10:28,078
700 billion dollars on capital projects in

261
00:10:28,078 --> 00:10:29,464
twenty twenty-six,

262
00:10:29,464 --> 00:10:32,179
much of it on AI data centers.

263
00:10:32,417 --> 00:10:34,617
That is also the risk.

264
00:10:34,897 --> 00:10:36,476
If that spending slows,

265
00:10:36,476 --> 00:10:38,147
so does Nvidia.

266
00:10:38,227 --> 00:10:40,610
Its biggest customers are building rival

267
00:10:40,610 --> 00:10:41,427
chips.

268
00:10:41,607 --> 00:10:43,703
And when a customer that large pauses

269
00:10:43,703 --> 00:10:44,521
its orders,

270
00:10:44,521 --> 00:10:47,427
it shows up directly in Nvidia's revenue.

271
00:10:47,747 --> 00:10:49,463
Its supply chain runs through a handful

272
00:10:49,463 --> 00:10:51,397
of factories in Asia.

273
00:10:51,617 --> 00:10:53,632
And export rules have shut much of

274
00:10:53,632 --> 00:10:55,019
the China market:

275
00:10:55,019 --> 00:10:57,929
Nvidia's latest forecast assumes no data

276
00:10:57,929 --> 00:11:00,544
center compute sales there at all.

277
00:11:00,783 --> 00:11:02,600
The market is shifting,

278
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:03,463
too.

279
00:11:03,673 --> 00:11:05,432
A model is trained once,

280
00:11:05,432 --> 00:11:07,880
but it runs every time someone uses

281
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:08,703
it.

282
00:11:08,843 --> 00:11:11,445
That second part is called inference,

283
00:11:11,445 --> 00:11:12,615
and as it grows,

284
00:11:12,615 --> 00:11:15,363
the cost of each answer matters more.

285
00:11:15,453 --> 00:11:17,466
That is exactly where custom chips

286
00:11:17,466 --> 00:11:18,513
compete.

287
00:11:18,733 --> 00:11:21,150
Nvidia's response is to keep moving,

288
00:11:21,150 --> 00:11:23,323
with new systems every year,

289
00:11:23,323 --> 00:11:24,763
like its next platform,

290
00:11:24,763 --> 00:11:25,471
